    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f9fabf12_e0daadd8",
        "filename": "apex/prebuilt.go",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 230,
      "author": {
        "id": 1039338
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-12-14T16:50:07Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I did consider that approach and in fact started implementing it that way but hit a few major issues that made me reevaluate my approach.\n\nDexpreopt (and boot jars package check) require that the boot images jars for art (e.g. core-oj, core-libart, etc.) are from the com.android.art APEX and ensures that by checking the ApexInfo associated with the APEX specific variant of the module.\n\nAn ApexInfo can only be associated with a variant by the apexInfoMutator(). Any attempt to use the ApexInfoProvider to set the value in another mutator causes a panic. That means that the ApexInfo for the prebuilt_apex needs to be associated with the java_import in the apexInfoMutator.\n\nAt this point I was stuck and thought about it some more and while I could probably make it work this way I decided to switch the dependencies from java_import-\u003eprebuilt_apex to prebuilt_apex-\u003ejava_import to make it as consistent with how \u0027apex\u0027 works as possible. That would make it easier to integrate into apexInfoMutator. It would also be consistent with how other \u0027aggregating\u0027 module types (i.e. sdk, sdk_snapshot) work.\n\nApexInfo has a lot of information that is provided by \u0027apex\u0027, while we do not appear to need \nany of that at the moment I wanted to make sure that we could provide it in future if it became necessary. If the ApexInfo was created by the java_import module then that information would either have to be duplicated in every java_import or the java_import would have to retrieve that information from the \"prebuilt_apex\" (requiring another Provider and probably yet another mutator).\n\nHaving the java_import depend on the \"prebuilt_apex\" would push all this logic down into that module type. If we needed to support another module type (e.g. java_sdk_library_import is a likely future candidate) then we would need to either duplicate the logic or more likely refactor that logic into some common base type (i.e. ModuleBase) which would be more complicated and make Soong even more entangled.\n\nHaving the logic here (with just a small amount of logic in each module type to access the exported files) makes things a littler simpler, albeit not actually simple.\n\nHaving dependencies in either direction causes some issues and there is no clear case that one is better than another and I thought in that regard consistency was the deciding factor.",
      "parentUuid": "24d7958d_87372b3c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 215,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 230,
        "endChar": 2
      },
      "revId": "c7e6e33247b0d8906d04c46194e3ea006f7e9b15",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a18166c1_a68db096",
